In article <4461.257c15e3@uwovax.uwo.ca> 7103_2622@uwovax.uwo.ca (Eric Smith) writes:
>In article <273@cmpfen.UUCP>, bob@cmpfen.UUCP (Bob Breum) writes:
>> When I play the Atari version, it seems that the creation of new items
>> is not entirely random, e.g., if I start with potions X and Y, and
>> rings A and B, then the odds are that I will find more of each of
>> these in the first level or two....
>
>The Atari version uses the random.c (Berkeley's random number generator)
>that came with NetHack, so this is probably just your imagination.

I have a feeling that it may seed random with something that doesn't change
very fast, though, like the higher end of the clock.  Reason I say that is
that I normally coldboot and then instantly run nethack, because I've 
usually got too much stuff in the machine for it to load.  I frequently
find myself in precisely the same starting position several such times
in a row.  (My battery-backed clock is temporarily dead, so I'm always
starting with raw wrong Atari time.  I think my movements in starting it
up are consistent enough that if the seed has a granularity of 1/2 second
or more, it could explain it.  Or, if it's seeding from 'time between
asking where the savefile is, and seeing my response' which was a favorite
trick on clockless PDP8's.  I invariably type a return before the question
comes up.)  I have certainly played the same level 1 several times in a
row, and can get the effect fairly consistently.

